Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe Model (TDMU) v2.342
—— A Cosmological Framework Based on Discrete Combinatorial Networks
Author: mjp

Date: August 2026

Abstract
The standard cosmological model (ΛCDM) relies on three exogenous assumptions—singularity, inflation, dark matter particles, and dark energy field—whose physical origins remain unexplained within known physics. This paper proposes an alternative framework: the universe is modeled as a topological interface (membrane) emerging from a discrete combinatorial network. The network consists of nodes and directed edges, with local reconnection as its sole dynamics.

Within this framework: space is the totality of distance relations among structures on the membrane; time is the cumulative count of reconnection events; matter consists of stable loop-weaved subgraphs (topological knots), whose recursive growth is truncated at the current energy scale; gravity is the statistical effect of the total number of shared links; light is a propagating transverse disturbance in the shared-link network, whose speed is determined by the intrinsic elastic properties of the membrane medium; nuclear transformations are governed by four channels of defect-complementary weaving and forced reconnection; elemental abundances are determined by a three-factor criterion (synthesis probability, continuation probability, and fission probability); celestial evolution is a dynamic interplay between Pathway I and Pathway II in accretion–dissipation cycles at different scales, with object types depending on total accreted mass and medium state.

This framework employs no singularity (replaced by a finite critical loop density), no inflation (replaced by initial conditions of membrane emergence), no dark matter particles (replaced by the shared-link convergence effect of supermassive topological stars), and no dark energy field (replaced by topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate). All observable cosmological phenomena—homogeneous background, large-scale structure, accelerated expansion, elemental abundance distribution—emerge from the same set of geometric rules.

This paper provides a complete system of postulates, core derivations, a celestial evolution hierarchy, and testable predictions.

1. Introduction
1.1 Exogenous Assumptions of the Standard Cosmological Model
The contemporary standard cosmological model (ΛCDM) has achieved remarkable observational success, yet its theoretical architecture relies on three exogenous assumptions:

Singularity: The universe originates from a mathematical singularity where spacetime curvature diverges. At this point, density, temperature, and curvature all tend to infinity, and general relativity breaks down. A physical theory that contains an indescribable singularity at its core indicates that the theory is incomplete.

Inflation: To explain the homogeneity and flatness of the universe, the standard model introduces a scalar field that underwent superluminal expansion in the very early universe. This field has no known physical counterpart, and its potential form requires artificial adjustment to match observational data.

Dark matter and dark energy: To explain galactic dynamics and cosmic accelerated expansion, the standard model introduces two unknown components, constituting 95% of the total energy of the universe. Dark matter particles have never been detected in terrestrial experiments; the physical nature of dark energy is completely unknown.

The common feature of these three assumptions is that they are not derived from deeper principles but are added layer by layer to fit observational data.

1.2 The Path of This Paper
The Topological Dynamic Membrane Universe Model (TDMU) starts from a minimal set of postulates: only nodes, directed edges, shared links, and reconnection counts. From these底层 definitions, all the above phenomena are derived or reformulated. The tasks of this paper are:

Present the complete postulate system (Parts I–II)

Establish the emergence mechanism for material structures (Part III)

Define the four channels of nuclear transformation and the three-factor criterion for elemental abundances (Parts IV–V)

Establish the geometry of shared links and the light–gravity decoupling (Part VI)

Construct the celestial evolution hierarchy and cosmological framework (Parts VII–VIII)

List testable predictions (Part IX)

Part I: Ontology
Postulate 1: Substrate

There exists a discrete, background-free combinatorial network ℳ. It consists of nodes and directed edges connecting nodes.

This network is not embedded in any space. It does not evolve in any pre-existing time. The only primitive relations between nodes and edges are "connected" or "not connected." The only primitive attribute of an edge is its direction—from one node to another.

The sole dynamics of the substrate is local reconnection: when two edges meet at a node, they may断开 and reconnect according to directional rules. Reconnection is local—it involves only the few edges sharing the same node, not distant structures.

Direction rule: On any closed path, when traversing along the path, edges in the direction of traversal are counted as forward, and edges opposite to traversal as backward. The algebraic sum of directedness of all edges is conserved under local reconnection operations.

The substrate contains no physical quantities. No mass, no energy, no charge, no field. Only nodes, directed edges, and their connectivity relations.

The substrate is not "nothingness"—it is a底层 existence with definite topological structure and dynamics. Nodes exist, directed edges exist, and local reconnection events occur continuously. The substrate is a never-ending topological sea, the raw material of all existence.

Definition 1.1: Loop

A closed path that starts from a node, follows directed edges, and eventually returns to the starting node is called a loop. Loops are the only structures in the substrate that possess "global recognizability."

Definition 1.2: Topological Charge

On any closed loop, the net algebraic sum of directions along the loop (forward-edge count minus backward-edge count) is an integer. This integer is conserved under reconnection operations—because the direction rule guarantees this.

This integer is called the topological charge of the loop.

Topological charge is not a pre-assumed physical quantity. It is merely a name for the pure geometric fact of "the algebraic sum of directions on a closed path."

Postulate 2: Spontaneous Emergence of Membranes

When, in some closed region of the substrate, the loop density (number of closed paths per unit node count) exceeds a certain critical value, the loops in that region tend, through collective reconnection, to weave themselves into a closed surface. This surface divides the region into "inside" and "outside"—the internal loop density differs from the external, and the surface itself becomes the boundary between the two regions.

This closed surface is called a membrane.

A membrane is not "placed into" the substrate. It is a structural pattern that the substrate naturally tends toward under specific loop-density conditions. A membrane is a state of the substrate—a phase-transition product from the non-weaved state to the weaved state. The emergence of a membrane instantly establishes an inside–outside gradient: the interior is organized into a weaved state, while the exterior remains in the non-weaved state. Once this gradient is established, the membrane possesses topological persistence and can exist as an independent universe.

Definition 2.1 (Inside–Outside Distinction of a Membrane): Let M be a connected subgraph of ℳ. The membrane M partitions ℳ into two disjoint node sets—the interior is the set of nodes connected to M but not to infinity, and the exterior is the set of nodes connected to M and also to infinity. The emergence of the membrane is the phase-transition moment at which ℳ first acquires this separative property.

Definition 2.2 (Ontological Status of the External Substrate): The external substrate and the internal substrate are ontologically the same entity—both are parts of ℳ. Both consist of nodes and directed edges, and both obey local reconnection dynamics. The difference lies only in the organizational mode: the membrane interior is in a weaved state, possessing stable loops, shared-link networks, and topological knots; the membrane exterior is in a non-weaved state, with loop density below the critical value, unable to form stable closed surfaces, and therefore lacking shared-link networks and topological knots. The external substrate is not nothingness—it possesses a definite state, but its dynamical mode is not directly coupled to the membrane interior.

Definition 2.3 (Topological Gradient Transition Zone, GTZ)

A membrane is not an infinitely thin geometric surface. The structure of a membrane is essentially a finite-thickness region of continuous transition from the internal weaved steady state to the external non-weaved state, called the Topological Gradient Transition Zone (GTZ).

