Part II: Emergence of Spacetime
Postulate 4: Combinatorial Distance

The distance between two nodes is defined by the number of edges on the shortest path connecting them. The distance between two structures is defined by the number of edges on the shortest path connecting their respective regions.

Distance is not a pre-existing metric in space. It is a direct reading of connectivity relations among nodes.

Postulate 5: Space

The totality of distance relations among all structures on the membrane constitutes the spatial structure of the membrane.

Space is not a container that holds the membrane. It is the set of relations among the structures on the membrane.

When a structure moves, it does not travel through space—it changes its connectivity relations with all other structures. "Motion" is a record in the reconnection history of changing distance relations.

Postulate 6: Time

The temporal order between two events A and B on the membrane is determined by the cumulative number of reconnection events on the paths connecting them. If the total number of reconnections on any path from A to B is N, with N > 0, then A precedes B; if N = 0, then A and B are simultaneous.

Time is not a dimension, not a coordinate, not a parameter. It is the cumulative count of reconnection events.

Part III: Emergence of Material Structures
Postulate 7: Topological Knot

A weaved subgraph on the membrane, composed of loops and not automatically dissociable under reconnection dynamics, is called a topological knot.

A subgraph is not automatically dissociable if and only if: the number of consecutive reconnections required to separate it from the rest of the membrane exceeds a threshold such that no local reconnection operation can release internal stress without destroying the overall structure.

Matter is not a pre-existing particle. It is a topological knot on the membrane that satisfies stability conditions.

Definition 7.1: Stability Parameters of a Topological Knot

The stability of a topological knot is determined by two factors:

Total internal links: the number of independent connection paths between loops within the knot. The larger this number, the more reconnections are required to dismantle it.

Boundary connectivity: the number of connection paths between the knot's surface and the exterior. Lower boundary connectivity makes it harder for external reconnections to invade the interior.

Definition 7.2: Topological Defects and Surface Weaving Imperfections

The surface weaving of any topological knot can never achieve absolutely perfect closure—there always exist regions that cannot be fully covered by the existing weave, called topological defects. The size and distribution of defects depend on the knot's weaving complexity: knots with few primitive bricks are loosely woven, with a larger fractional defect area; knots with many primitive bricks are tightly woven, with a smaller fractional defect area.

Topological defects are "active sites" for interactions between topological knots. Whether two knots can undergo defect-complementary weaving depends entirely on the geometric matching of their surface defects.

Definition 7.2a (Scale Properties of Topological Defects): Topological defects differ only in size and distribution patterns; there is no absolutely defect-free perfect topological knot. Light nuclei are loosely woven with larger defects; heavy nuclei are tightly woven with smaller defects. Defect size decreases monotonically with nuclear mass but never vanishes.

Definition 7.3: Primitive Brick

The smallest topological knot on the membrane that can exist independently and establish stable shared links with other topological knots is called a primitive brick.

The primitive brick is the effective elementary unit at the current energy scale. Its "elementarity" is scale-dependent—if future observations at higher energy scales reveal that primitive bricks are composed of smaller structures, the model can be extended naturally.

Definition 7.4: Inertia of a Primitive Brick

The ability of a primitive brick to resist directional reconnection—that is, the number of reconnection events required to change the net algebraic sum of its loop directions—is defined as its inertia. In the phenomenological layer, this property is called "mass."

Postulate 8: Recursive Growth and Topological Knot Hierarchy

Topological knots do not exist in isolation. Under appropriate energy scales and collision conditions, smaller stable topological knots can combine into larger stable knots through the defect-complementary weaving mechanism. When the defects of two smaller knots are geometrically complementary, they interlock to form a larger weaved structure—this process is determined entirely by the geometric properties of their respective defects.

The smaller knots that can stably combine into larger knots must satisfy certain topological compatibility conditions on their defect distributions. This naturally yields a recursive growth tree of topological knots—starting from primitive bricks, combining generation by generation, forming stable knots of increasing complexity and inertia.

Energy-Scale Cutoff Rule for Recursive Growth

Recursive growth of topological knots does not proceed indefinitely. At a given loop density (corresponding to the phenomenological energy scale or temperature), only topological knots with inertia below a certain threshold can exist stably. When recursive growth reaches the maximum stable weaving complexity at that energy scale, more complex topological knots cannot form due to insufficient loop density support, or they are immediately disrupted by thermal fluctuations.

At the current low energy scale of the universe (about 2.7 K CMB), recursive growth is truncated at the baryon-brick level (protons and neutrons). The internal structure of primitive bricks—if it exists—is indecomposable at the current energy scale, so primitive bricks appear as "elementary particles" at the phenomenological level.

The nature of this cutoff is the same as the hierarchical cutoff criterion (Corollary 3.1): when the weaving complexity exceeds the stability threshold of the shared-link network sustainable by the current loop density, higher-order topological knots cannot emerge.

Definition 8.1: Electron Brick

In the recursive growth tree, a stable topological knot composed of 4 primitive bricks in a specific weaving configuration is called an electron brick. It is the smallest independent stable knot at the current cosmic energy scale and serves as the fundamental building block for all more complex material structures.

The internal structure of an electron brick also follows the recursive weaving principle: it possesses a solid core formed by the tight weaving of those 4 primitive bricks, and possibly a soft outer layer composed of lower-level primitive bricks attached to the core through defects. At the current low energy scale of the universe, the inertia contribution of this outer layer is negligibly small.

