Soft outer layer: composed of a large number of primitive bricks or small topological knots loosely attached to the core surface through defect-complementary adhesion, forming a deformable buffer layer. The outer-layer primitive bricks can slide under external forces, absorbing collision energy. The defect distribution of the outer layer determines the macroscopic charge manifestation of the topological knot.

These two stable configurations correspond phenomenologically to the proton (positively charged) and the neutron (electrically neutral). They share the same structural pattern, differing only in the net topological-charge property of the outer-layer defects.

