Part IV: Nuclear Transformations—The Four Reaction Channels
4.1 Matter: Stable Topological Knots and Topological Defects
Matter particles are stable topological knots on the membrane. Every topological knot carries regions where the weave cannot be completely closed—topological defects. Defects differ only in size and distribution patterns; there is no absolutely defect-free perfect knot. Light nuclei are loosely woven with larger defects; heavy nuclei are tightly woven with smaller defects.

4.2 Reaction Scheme and the Four Mechanisms
Interactions between topological knots are essentially the complementary matching of surface defects and the reconfiguration of connecting edges. The complete scheme for a nuclear reaction is written as:

