Channel I: Defect- Complementary Weaving (Pathway I)

The essential feature of Pathway I is defect-induced, stepwise reconnection.

Condition: the topological defects on the surfaces of two topological knots are geometrically complementary.

Process: the defect regions interlock, and the connecting edges are restructured stepwise in an ordered manner, forming a new stable topological knot 
x
x. The energy released depends on the total inertia deficit of the involved knots.

Characteristics:

The aggregation probability of Pathway I depends on the geometric matching degree of the surface defects. As nuclear mass increases, the weave becomes tighter and the fractional defect area decreases monotonically—so the aggregation probability qualitatively tends to decrease with increasing mass. However, this decrease is not to zero; any two topological knots have a nonzero aggregation probability.

In environments with sufficient heat dissipation and thermal expansion room, Pathway I can form a negative-feedback self-stabilizing loop: local temperature rise → expansion → defect separation increases → matching probability decreases → heat release automatically reduces. This is the mechanism behind the sustained mild burning in stellar interiors for billions of years.

Negative feedback is not automatically guaranteed but is a product of specific environmental conditions. It requires the medium to have rapid heat dissipation (e.g., fluid convection) and immediate thermal expansion response (density decreasing rapidly with temperature rise). Gaseous and high-temperature molten celestial bodies satisfy these conditions, so Pathway I can operate stably inside them. Solid bodies lack both—but if the interior of a solid body heats up to melting, the medium acquires fluid properties, and negative feedback can be established.

The aggregation path (sequential or leapfrog) is dynamically determined by the abundance ratio of available raw materials.

Channel II: Forced Chain Reconnection (Pathway II)

The essential feature of Pathway II is bulk shearing and collective restructuring—independent of defect matching probability, without selectivity, and once triggered, it proceeds inevitably, unmodulable by environmental feedback.

Condition: the number of local reconnection operations exceeds the bulk fracture threshold of connecting edges, i.e., the external compression energy exceeds the compressive binding energy 
