4.4 Deep Mechanism of the Coulomb Barrier
Whether two topological knots can interlock depends on the configurational matching of their surface defects. Increasing temperature means larger thermal vibration amplitudes, allowing defect sites to be more fully exposed, increasing the matching probability. The Coulomb barrier, as measured macroscopically, is the statistical failure rate of countless defect-matching attempts. It is not an independent force, but a macroscopic statistical manifestation of probability.

Part V: Elemental Abundances and the Iron Peak—Three-Factor Criterion
5.1 The Three-Factor Criterion
In the reaction network formed by the four channels, the abundance of any nuclide in the universe is determined jointly by the following three probabilities:

Synthesis probability: the probability that the nuclide is produced through various precursor channels. If a channel is blocked by Pathway III, that contribution is zero, but the nuclide may still be synthesized through other channels.

Continuation probability: the probability that the nuclide, as a reactant, continues to aggregate into heavier nuclides via Pathway I or Pathway II.

Fission probability: the probability that the nuclide fissions back into lighter nuclei due to internal stress rupture.

These three jointly determine the net retention of the nuclide on the mass spectrum:

Net retention
∝
Synthesis probability
×
(
1
−
Continuation probability
−
Fission probability
)
Net retention∝Synthesis probability×(1−Continuation probability−Fission probability)
Abundance peaks occur at nuclides where "synthesis probability is appreciable, but both continuation probability and fission probability are low"—nucleonic flow can reach there, but once there, it neither easily continues forward nor is easily pushed back, so net accumulation occurs. Abundance valleys occur at nuclides where "synthesis probability is low" or "continuation probability is high" or "fission probability is high"—nucleonic flow either cannot reach there, or cannot stay.

The entire elemental abundance distribution is the integrated projection of these three probabilities varying continuously across the mass spectrum.

5.2 Leapfrog Paths versus Sequential Paths
In this framework, there is no "mandatory route" on the nuclear mass spectrum. Any two topological knots (light, medium, or heavy) can aggregate as long as their defects match. The reaction probability depends on the geometric matching degree of their defects, with no rigid constraint from their positions on the mass spectrum.

Leapfrog paths and sequential paths are not mutually exclusive; they are two parallel channels whose weights are dynamically determined by the abundance ratio of raw materials. In the early universe, when material abundances were uniform, leapfrog paths became the dominant channel, exponentially accelerating structural growth.

5.3 The Iron Peak: A Specific Manifestation of the Three-Factor Criterion
The prominence of iron-56 is a specific manifestation of the above three-factor criterion at mass number 56:

Synthesis probability: nucleonic flow can reach mass number 56 through both sequential and leapfrog paths.

Continuation probability: the defect area fraction of the mass-56 topological knot is extremely small, so its probability of continuing to aggregate upward as a reactant is low—the nucleonic flow "moves forward" slowly here.

Fission probability: the internal suture-line stresses of the mass-56 topological knot are well balanced, so its probability of being torn back to lighter nuclei is also low—the nucleonic flow "pushed backward" also moves slowly here.

The superposition of these three: nucleonic flow can reach mass number 56, but once there, both the forward and backward rates are low—resulting in net accumulation. This is the entire cause of the iron peak. As for which specific isotope of mass number 56 remains, that is determined by the specific fission probability of that isotope, and does not affect the fact that the iron peak is a mass-number abundance peak.

The iron peak has no causal relation with stellar evolution. The prominence of iron in abundance is merely a large net accumulation of nucleonic flow at that mass number. It does not determine any evolutionary turning point of a star. A star can trigger Pathway II at any stage—independent of which mass number happens to form an abundance peak.

5.4 Astrophysical Role of Iron Nuclei
Abundance level: the position of the iron peak is determined by the three-factor criterion.

