5.6 Geometric Necessity of Light-Nucleus Enrichment and Its Geological Consequences
The aggregation probability of Pathway I depends on the geometric matching degree of the surface defects. Light nuclei (carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, etc.) are loosely woven with larger defect area fractions, so their matching probability for Pathway I aggregation is qualitatively higher than that of heavy nuclei. At the same time, the initial particle-number base of light nuclei in the solar system is much larger than that of heavy nuclei. The superposition of these two—high matching probability and large base—makes the total synthesis of light nuclides in the Pathway I aggregation–fission cycle far greater than that of heavy nuclides. This is the geometric origin of the high cosmic abundance of life-essential elements such as carbon and oxygen, requiring no special mechanisms.

Inside the Earth, carbon and hydrogen continuously synthesized by Pathway I in the core upwell to the crust through mantle plumes and deep fluid channels. The enormous supply of these light nuclides provides the material prerequisite for abiotic chemical synthesis of hydrocarbons in the crust. TDMU itself does not describe chemical bonding processes, but it provides a nucleosynthesis-level geometric explanation for the deep inorganic origin of hydrocarbons. This is qualitatively compatible with observations of certain ultra-deep oil reservoirs lacking biomarkers and their association with mantle-derived fluids with elevated 
