Specifically:

Propagation medium: Light requires a medium, namely the shared-link network permeating the cosmic membrane. Regions without a shared-link network (such as beyond the outer boundary of the GTZ) contain no light.

What vibrates: What vibrates is not electric or magnetic fields, but the spatial orientation arrangement of shared links. When shared links exhibit a statistically nonuniform spatial orientation, magnetism (static torsional stress field) arises; when this nonuniformity propagates as a transverse wave along the shared-link network, it manifests as light.

Photon: There is no independent photon particle. A "photon" is a local excitation of the discrete phase field 
