6.7 Geometric Definitions of the Five Fundamental Interactions
Definition 9.4: Gravity

The greater the total number of shared links between two topological knots, the stronger the tendency of the membrane to reduce the overall stress—this tendency manifests at the phenomenological level as pulling the two knots toward each other. Gravity is the large-number statistical effect of the total shared-link count. No graviton is required.

Definition 9.5: Magnetism

When shared links exhibit a statistically nonuniform spatial orientation distribution, a macroscopic torsional stress field is generated. The streamlines of this stress field are magnetic field lines. The closure of magnetic field lines is topologically necessary—shared links cannot have isolated endpoints in space. Magnetic monopoles are topologically forbidden.

Definition 9.6: Electricity

The density gradient of shared links generates normal stress waves. Between regions of excess and deficit density, there are stress differences:

Excess–excess (or deficit–deficit) → stress difference is repulsive

Excess–deficit → stress difference is attractive

Electricity is the orthogonal deformation mode of magnetism—together they constitute the complete linear response of shared-link disturbances.

Definition 9.7: Strong Nuclear Force

When the distance between two topological knots shrinks to the point where the saturation of shared links approaches the maximum:

Shared links form an inseparable shared network → disassembly requires simultaneously cutting a large number of links → attraction

If the distance shrinks further, the shared-link paths are forced to bend → generating repulsive bow-string stress → repulsion

The strong nuclear force is the nonlinear behavior of shared links at extremely short ranges.

Definition 9.8: Weak Interaction

When the distance between two topological knots is in the critical range where shared links "can just barely be established," the number of shared links is extremely small. This few-link state is metastable—any local reconnection perturbation may break the links and trigger a cascade of disintegration. The weak interaction is not a "force," but a spontaneous decay mode of the metastable shared-link network.

Definition 9.9: Phenomenological Electric Current

In a conductor, the shared links on the surfaces of the nuclear topological knots, in the absence of an external field, are distributed along closed paths—this is the lowest-energy mode of shared links in cylindrical geometry. An external field (the density gradient of shared links) stretches these closed paths into helical paths along the conductor direction. The helically advancing shared links drag the surrounding electron bricks to move synchronously. Electric current is the collective manifestation of the helical motion of shared links. Resistance is the statistical frequency of collisions and reconnections of shared links in motion.

Definition 9.10: Superconductivity

When the helical motion of shared links reaches perfect synchronization—all links have identical phase and collision frequency drops to zero—shared links enter a dissipationless transmission state. Superconductivity is a synchronous phase transition of the collective motion mode of shared links.

Part VII: Life Cycle of the Universe and Celestial Evolution Hierarchy
Postulate 10: Membrane Emergence Event

In some region of the substrate, the boundary conditions reach the topological bifurcation threshold, and the loop-density distribution function 
