This event is observed at the phenomenological level as the "beginning" of the universe, but at the bottom level of this framework there is no temporal zero point—membrane emergence is a phase transition of the substrate from one topological boundary condition to another. Time (reconnection counting) begins to accumulate only after the S-shaped profile is formed.

Intuitive dynamical picture: a boundless, thick, liquid-like substrate, whose local boundary conditions reach a critical phase-transition threshold, gives birth to an expanding and eventually dissipating topological bubble. This is the membrane emergence event of the universe.

Postulate 11: Evolution

After formation, the membrane continues to expand. Expansion causes the overall loop density on the membrane to decrease—phenomenologically called "cooling." Temperature is the local manifestation of the loop-density gradient.

During cooling, topological knots emerge sequentially: primitive bricks → electron bricks → leptons → mesons → baryons → atomic nuclei → atoms → molecules.

Each step is the most stable weaving structure "permitted to exist" at the current loop density. As the loop density continues to decrease, recursive growth is truncated at the baryon-brick level—higher-order topological knots cannot exist stably due to insufficient loop-density support (see Part III, "Energy-Scale Cutoff Rule for Recursive Growth").

The current membrane is in an intermediate state where "loop density is low enough, but not yet low enough for membrane dissolution."

Postulate 12: Final State

The membrane remains part of the substrate throughout. The dissolution of the membrane is not driven by "external circulation pressure decaying over time," but by the instability of the S-shaped profile of the GTZ.

