 ). The former corresponds to a dense, high-temperature envelope; the latter to a tenuous, low-temperature envelope.

The three paths are not strictly isolated. Changes in external matter supply during the star's lifetime can cause migration between paths. A main-sequence star (Path B) with a sudden increase in accretion rate can enter the Camouflage Path (Path C); a camouflaged star, after envelope dissipation, can re-emerge as a normal main-sequence star or even degrade to a smoldering object.

Common Subsequent Evolution—Envelope Phase:

Regardless of the initial path, as long as the star possesses an envelope, it enters the envelope evolution phase. The envelope consists of accreted material, with light nuclei dominating its surface layer, where Pathway I defect-complementary weaving dominates. Because the envelope is a fluid medium, Pathway I possesses negative-feedback self-stabilization: local heat release → expansion → density decrease → defect separation increases → matching probability decreases → heat release automatically reduces → cooling contraction → density recovery → matching probability restored.

During this phase, a branch event may occur: localized Pathway II outburst. When the accretion compression rate exceeds the 
