  of local envelope nuclides, Pathway II is triggered inside the envelope. Because the envelope matter is weakly gravitationally bound, the outburst is usually confined to flares, pulsations, or localized mass ejections, without destroying the core structure. Observational counterparts: stellar flares, non-radial pulsations, some novae or dwarf novae.

When external matter supply declines, the envelope gradually dissipates, and Pathway I burning weakens, decreasing luminosity. After the envelope is completely depleted, the core becomes exposed. The nature of the core depends on the initial path and its evolutionary history:

If the core mass is below the black-hole threshold (from low-mass main-sequence stars in Path B), it becomes a degenerate thermal remnant—a white dwarf. Pathway I in the white-dwarf core is nearly frozen due to insufficient density to sustain high-frequency collisions, and it emits only residual heat, cooling slowly. If the white dwarf is in a binary system and accretes matter from a companion, its mass grows toward the critical point, Pathway I is reactivated, and localized Pathway II is triggered; because the degenerate state lacks negative feedback, the entire star undergoes instantaneous chain reconnection—a Type Ia supernova, complete disruption with no remnant.

If the core mass exceeds the black-hole threshold (from Path C, or from massive main-sequence stars in Path B whose cores cross the critical density), it becomes a superfluid vortex condensate—a black-hole-grade topological star (phenomenological "black hole"). This enters the exposed black-hole phase, observationally corresponding to a hungry bare nucleus (e.g., Sgr A*). If external matter is supplied again, the star can re-enter the envelope evolution phase, forming a cycle.

Common Subsequent Evolution—Decoherence Phase:

After the black hole is exposed, it faces two branching fates.

Mainstream path—silent decoherence: the vortex twist energy slowly leaks through background longitudinal waves, and the superfluid state smoothly dissolves over extremely long time scales, with the black-hole mass gradually decreasing. When the mass drops to the critical value below which global phase locking 
