Low-probability branch—unstable explosion (core-collapse supernova): during decoherence, local fluctuations in the vortex network trigger a chain of Pathway II events, and the total released energy exceeds the residual gravitational binding energy. This is the product of three independent probabilities—local fluctuation probability × chain propagation probability × binding-overcoming probability—far less than 1. When the conditions are satisfied, the entire vortex core disintegrates, and the frozen vortex twist energy is released in an extremely short time. Observational counterparts: Type II, Type Ib/c core-collapse supernovae. Ejecta return to the nebula, closing the cycle. The vast majority of black holes transition smoothly to the weaved solid state through silent decoherence; supernovae are a rare complication of decoherence.

Final destination—weaved solid remnants:

The weaved solid state continues to cool, Pathway II permanently ceases, and only the unobservable extremely low-rate Pathway I continues. The object emits no light, becoming a cold solid core.

Complete cycle closure:

At any stage of the evolutionary tree, changes in external matter supply can cause path migration. If a weaved solid object is in a binary system, accreting companion matter can increase its mass and reactivate it. Supernova ejecta return to the nebula, initiating a new round of gravitational condensation. There is no true "final state," only dynamic switching between Pathway I and Pathway II at different stages and scales. Stars are transient states in the accretion–dissipation cycle.

7.3 Compact Topological Stars (Pulsars): Pathway I Accumulation → Pathway II Periodic Triggering
When the energy released by Pathway II is insufficient to completely blow the object apart, the remnant is compressed to the extreme by gravity. In this extreme compression state, the spherical knots are squeezed to the limit, surface structures collapse—defect area fraction approaches 100%, and the entire object is in a "fully surface-reconnectable" state.

Definition of a compact topological star: a dense aggregate of topological knots compressed so that defects approach 100% of the surface, with no distinction of "stable nuclides." In this compression limit, the surface weaving of the topological knots has been completely destroyed, and all nodes are in a connection-saturated state—every edge in the network has reached its maximum coordination number and cannot accept new connections. This is connection-saturated rigidity—the topological counterpart of phenomenological "degeneracy pressure."

Pulsar radiation mechanism—stress accumulation–trigger–release cycle:

Phase 1: Pathway I continues to operate in the solid network. A tiny fraction of nuclides independently reach reaction conditions locally, releasing minute amounts of heat and stress. The total number of particles is enormous, and the cumulative effect of these events continuously drives the stress in the central region toward the 
