Phase 3: The shock wave propagates through the solid network toward the entire spherical surface. The wave front is extremely narrow, and the duration at any location is extremely short. When the shock wave reaches the surface layer, the wave front compresses the surface nuclides, exceeding their instantaneous tolerance limit, producing a "shattering" effect and ejecting charged fragments. Observers, being extremely far from the object, receive signals only from a very narrow line of sight.

Phase 4: After the pulse release, the object returns to Pathway I and begins accumulating stress again, forming a cycle.

