Quasars do not pass through a phenomenological main-sequence stellar progenitor stage. In the complete TDMU life-cycle picture, quasars correspond to the "fully active" phase—their cores are already formed compact topological stars, with envelopes being thick, high-accretion-rate accretion layers.

7.6 Shared-Link Origin of Planetary Magnetic Fields
The generation of planetary magnetic fields does not require specific material compositions, but is a geometric result of the shared-link network achieving large-scale directional alignment within the planet. Pathway I in the planetary core continuously releases heat, driving convective motion in the liquid region. The planet's rotation acts as a global inertial mold, forcing the random thermal convection into large-scale toroidal vortices aligned with the rotation axis—this macroscopic ordered flow is equivalent to large-scale directional alignment of shared links. According to Definition 9.5, this directional alignment closes on the core scale, emerging as a stable dipolar magnetic field.

The magnetic polarity corresponds to the chirality (left-handed or right-handed helical threading) of the shared-link network. Geomagnetic reversals are global chirality phase transitions of the outer-core circulation across topological bifurcation points—when the heat output of Pathway I accumulates asymmetrically between the northern and southern hemispheres, the asymmetric perturbation is amplified by rotation, forcing the entire shared-link network's chirality to flip. The irregularity of reversal intervals arises from local Pathway II events randomly injecting reverse seed fiber bundles into the circulation. The slow drift of magnetic poles is the meridional precession of the fluid symmetry axis.

The unifying test of this mechanism: Venus has a mass similar to Earth but an extremely slow rotation (243 days); the lack of rotational constraint means that fluid motion is merely local random turbulence, and shared links cannot close on large scales, so there is no internal dipole magnetic field. Jupiter's metallic hydrogen generates a magnetic field, proving that magnetism does not depend on iron–nickel composition—the nature of the magnetic field is directional alignment of shared links, not specific matter.

Part VIII: Geometric Explanations of Cosmological Observations
8.1 Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation
The cosmic microwave background radiation is the topological snapshot left at the critical transition moment when the membrane interface went from violent change to equilibrium—i.e., the instant when weaving structures were frozen.

Homogeneous background: the geometric mean state of the initial conditions of membrane emergence. No inflation is needed to explain homogeneity; homogeneity is the starting point of membrane emergence.

Temperature fluctuations (anisotropy): statistical fluctuations in local shared-link density on the interface, locked at the freeze-out moment. Hot spots correspond to slightly higher density, cold spots to slightly lower density.

Multipole structure of the angular power spectrum: the spatial projection statistics of topological structures of different scales on the interface at the freeze-out moment.

8.2 Large-Scale Structure
The initial homogeneity inside the membrane is broken by statistical fluctuations in local reconnection. The fluctuations are amplified by the positive feedback of Pathway I. Leapfrog paths allow cross-order aggregation, accelerating structure formation.

No dark-matter halos are needed as gravitational seeds.

