8.7 Topological Coupling Mechanism of Multiverse Membranes
The ontological status of the substrate (Postulate 1) implies that membranes are not the only possible structures. If the loop density in some region of the substrate exceeds the critical value, a membrane can emerge spontaneously. This means that multiple membranes—multiple universes—may exist in the substrate, each emerging independently and each possessing its own internal weaved state and shared-link network.

8.7.1 Fundamental Constraints on Inter-Membrane Interactions
The substrate region between two membranes is in a non-weaved state. In the non-weaved substrate, there is no shared-link network, so light (
δ
A
δA) cannot propagate between membranes. There is no "optical communication" between membranes in the usual sense.

However, this does not mean that membranes are completely isolated from each other. The non-weaved substrate still consists of nodes and directed edges, and still obeys local reconnection dynamics. Any violent dynamical process in one membrane generates local reconnection disturbances in the substrate, which propagate as reconnection-event waves through the substrate and may reach another membrane.

8.7.2 Three Channels of Inter-Membrane Interaction
Channel 1: Direct impact of substrate reconnection shock waves

Any violent dynamical process in an external membrane—its internal Pathway II events (supernova explosions or core collapses), or global oscillations of the membrane itself—generates local reconnection disturbances in the substrate. These disturbances are not light, but reconnection-event waves of the substrate.

When such a reconnection-event wave reaches the surface of our membrane, it interacts locally with the topological defects (Definition 7.2) on the membrane surface. Because the membrane surface is the interface between the weaved and non-weaved states, surface topological defects are the only sites that can interact with external disturbances. Observable manifestations of this interaction include:

Collective excitation of surface defects, manifesting as coherent anomalies in the cosmic microwave background that cannot be explained by internal fluctuations

Energy injection in specific directions, possibly triggering local weaving reconstruction on the membrane surface, manifesting as diffuse gamma-ray bursts or X-ray flashes of unknown origin

It should be noted that reconnection-event waves in the non-weaved substrate, lacking a coherent medium, disperse rapidly, with intensity decaying exponentially with distance. This means that only external membranes sufficiently close to ours could produce observable impact events.

Channel 2: Indirect influence of topological tension coupling

There exists topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate (an extension of Postulate 5). If another massive membrane exists externally, its own topological tension modifies the substrate stress field in our membrane's vicinity.

The anisotropy of the substrate stress field would cause an anisotropic accelerated expansion rate in our universe—systematic differences in the Hubble constant in different directions. These differences should not be random but should exhibit large-scale dipole or quadrupole patterns correlated with the direction of the external membrane.

Current cosmological observations have revealed the so-called "Hubble tension"—significant discrepancies in Hubble constant values measured by different methods. TDMU attributes this to anisotropic stress from external topological structures, rather than measurement systematics or new physics. This interpretation gives a clear observational prediction: the directional dependence of the Hubble constant should exhibit systematic patterns, not random distributions.

Channel 3: Topological defect handshake between membranes—an extremely rare large-scale coupling

If two membranes approach so closely that their surface topological defects come into direct contact, a defect-complementary weaving between the two membrane surfaces may occur.

Defect-complementary weaving is the fundamental mechanism for interactions between topological knots in TDMU (Pathway I). The same mechanism can act between the surface defects of two membranes. When the defects on the two membrane surfaces are geometrically complementary, they interlock to form a shared-link "bridge" connecting the two membranes.

The establishment of this bridge is an instantaneous phase-transition event. After that, for the first time, a shared-link pathway appears between the two membranes. Light (
δ
A
δA) can propagate from one membrane to the other along this bridge. Such an event might manifest at the phenomenological level as:

An extremely high-energy radiation burst in some region of the universe, appearing in a very short time and inexplicable by any known astrophysical process

Accompanying the burst, a transient, local, measurable deviation of the fine-structure constant or the speed of light—because the shared-link networks of the two membranes briefly overlap, changing the local shared-link network properties

If the bridge is stable, it becomes a permanent channel connecting the two membranes. At the phenomenological level, this corresponds to the concept of a wormhole in standard cosmology, but the ontological basis is completely different: the wormhole is not a connectivity of spacetime topology, but a shared-link bridge between two weaved membranes.

8.7.3 Multiverse Membranes and the Ultimate Constraint on Civilization Survival
The multi-membrane coupling mechanism of TDMU provides a physical formulation for an ancient philosophical question: can a civilization survive after its parent membrane dissolves?

From Postulate 12, any membrane—whether naturally emerged or artificially constructed—will eventually dissolve. The dissolution of a membrane means the collapse of all weaved structures inside the membrane, including all topological knots, all shared-link networks, and all matter and life based on these structures. There are no exceptions.

Therefore, for a civilization to surpass the lifetime limit of a single membrane, it must master inter-membrane migration technology. This includes two possible paths:

Path 1: Construct an artificial membrane (an Ark). Before the parent membrane dissolves, the civilization can use the matter and energy inside the parent membrane to construct a sub-membrane that can independently maintain weaving stability. The sub-membrane survives the impact wave of the parent membrane's dissolution, then navigates through the substrate to find another natural membrane and establish a shared-link bridge with it. The technical challenges of this path have been discussed in Part VII.

Path 2: Find an existing external membrane and establish a connection in advance. If the civilization can detect a neighboring external membrane and establish a shared-link bridge with it before the parent membrane dissolves, it can transfer its population and information to the external membrane at the moment of dissolution. This path avoids the engineering challenge of constructing a sub-membrane, but requires extremely stringent conditions: the external membrane must be sufficiently close, its weaving pattern must be compatible with the parent membrane, and its surface defects must be geometrically complementary to those of the parent membrane.

Whichever path is chosen, civilization faces a fundamental limitation: there is no shared-link network in the substrate, so light cannot propagate between membranes. During inter-membrane migration, the civilization will be in a complete information blind zone—they cannot perceive the external environment through light, cannot know the internal state of the destination membrane in advance, and cannot communicate with any external entity during the voyage. They can only perceive the world by touching the tremors of the substrate itself.

This is the deepest constraint that TDMU imposes on civilization: membranes are oases of existence; the substrate is a boundless desert. The journey across the desert is blind, solitary, and irreversible. The ultimate fate of a civilization depends not on its degree of development inside a single membrane, but on whether it can learn to continuously migrate across generations of membranes—building the next Ark and finding the next home before each membrane dissolves.

There is no end. Only cycles. Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection is relentless.

