Prediction 9.6: Anisotropic expansion

Prediction: If the Hubble constant exhibits systematic differences beyond statistical errors in different sky regions, and the difference distribution shows large-scale dipole or quadrupole patterns, it may indicate topological tension coupling from external cosmic membranes. If the directional dependence of the Hubble constant disappears at higher precision, this prediction is falsified.

Prediction 9.7: Transient signals from inter-membrane bridging

Prediction: If an extremely high-energy radiation burst is observed in the universe that cannot be explained by any known astrophysical process, and this burst is accompanied by transient, local, measurable deviations of the fine-structure constant or the speed of light, it may correspond to a topological defect handshake between two membranes. If all such bursts are not accompanied by such transient deviations, then this channel is not activated in the observational window.

Part X: Reflection
All content of physics—space, time, matter, gravity, magnetism, electricity, nuclear forces, weak force, electric current, superconductivity, nuclear transformations, elemental abundances, celestial evolution, the topological phase-transition origin and final state of membranes, the geometric enforcement of quantum statistics, the geometric decoupling of isotopic fission, the topological origin of gauge fields in superconductivity—all reduce to three things:

Nodes, directed edges, shared links.

Plus two emergence principles:

Recursive weaving and defect complementarity.

And two cutoff rules:

the hierarchical cutoff criterion (the effective interaction window is determined by the recursive growth radius) and the energy-scale cutoff criterion (recursive growth is truncated at the highest stable weaving complexity sustainable at the current loop density).

Theorem 8.1 re-anchors Pauli exclusion—one of the most central statistical rules in quantum field theory—from the mysterious outcome of the spin–statistics theorem to the geometric enforcement of braided exchange. Two identical topological knots cannot occupy exactly the same geometric state, not because they obey Fermi–Dirac statistics, but because exchanging their ports requires an odd number of reconnections, while the identity operation requires zero steps (even)—the contradiction that an odd number equals an even number forbids complete state coincidence at the purely combinatorial level.

There are no absolute "elementary particles," only eternal recursion and emergence—where recursion is truncated by energy scale, that is where "elementary particles" appear. There is no true "final state," only the dynamic switching between Pathway I and Pathway II in accretion–dissipation cycles. Stars are not one-way arrows toward an end, but transient states in a cyclic network—brown dwarfs, main-sequence stars, extreme stars, white dwarfs, black holes, pulsars, supernovae—all are different projections of the same set of geometric mechanisms onto the three coordinates of total accreted mass, medium state, and evolutionary stage.

In Part VIII, we go further: our membrane—our universe—may not be the only self-organizing vortex in the substrate. There may be other membranes, each with its own internal spacetime and physics, each undergoing emergence and dissolution. There is no optical communication between membranes—light is but a ripple on the shared-link network inside a membrane, unable to traverse the non-weaved substrate. Yet they are not completely isolated from each other. The tremors of the substrate itself convey their presence. If a membrane forms or dies nearby, our universe will feel a silent ripple on the topological sea—not seen in light, but heard in the rhythm of expansion.

At the bottom of a summer pond, a toad lies still in the water, blowing bubbles. The bubbles rise, expand, deform, oscillate, and finally burst, returning to the water waves.

In this imagery:

The water is the substrate—the invisible, all-bearing foundation. There may be other bubbles in the water, each rising, expanding, and bursting independently. Bubbles do not transmit sound directly to each other, but they are all in the same water, and their tremors are transmitted through the water.

The bubble wall is the cosmic membrane—the ordered structure emerging from the substrate.

The rising of the bubble is time—irreversible unfolding.

The expansion of the bubble is the cooling and evolution of the universe.

The texture on the bubble is matter—the weaved structures on the membrane, whose complexity is truncated by the current energy scale.

The bursting of the bubble is the final state—structural dissolution, returning to the substrate.

The toad still lies quietly at the unreachable depth of the substrate. We are born on the ripples of the membrane and, in our entire lives, cannot look back at it—but that does not prevent us, through the topological imprints on the membrane, from gradually reconstructing its image.

Why do I keep this imagery? The toad blowing bubbles was the starting point of my model. Through multiple rounds of iterative deepening, I cannot abandon it. Without this imagery, none of this would exist. So I still call it the Toad Universe Model. And my topological stars—I prefer to call them Toad Stars.

Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection is relentless.

